r thing  viola was like I really want to make  sure that at the end of the story  there's a form of closure but it's not  over it's like what you just talked  about it just that that pain goes  somewhere you put it somewhere you deal  with it but it's not like it's gone so  she drives off in the sunset so to speak  but it's actually kind of a haunting  sunset so yeah absolutely and there's a  responsibility when telling stories like  this to be authentic to the victim  experience into the victims family  experience and we don't really see that  very often in film so it's you know the  scenes where you're in the hospital the  scenes where you're you're grappling  with the memories and stuff the often we  don't see that in movies we see the rage  in this where the kind of Charles  Bronson approach yeah with the woman  story you really had to add new nuances  or else it would just feel like a dress  you know yeah because Charles Bronson  can get a gun and just shoot people and  people like yeah that's right you know  when a woman does it it's like oh yeah  but can't you be a little nice about it  you know and um so I mean I mean there  is an unapologetic aspect of it and what  I liked about it if  if I can be so bold to say is it wasn't  like after every killing she explored  any kind of regret that the exploration  really was how can I get over my pain  not how can I get over the fact that I  killed people too you know and I kind of  like that with girls in it I do it's  it's there's something very liberating  about it at the end of the day
 

